1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,250
In August 2001, Microsoft
released Internet Explorer 6.

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,610
The next version of the browser
came out in October 2006.

3
00:00:11,058 --> 00:00:12,728
Five years and two months.

4
00:00:13,618 --> 00:00:15,878
In between, no new version
arrived for the browser

5
00:00:15,958 --> 00:00:18,468
that almost everyone
in the world was using.

6
00:00:21,028 --> 00:00:23,238
And it wasn't because nobody complained.

7
00:00:24,348 --> 00:00:27,528
By the middle of that gap there
were whole websites devoted to what

8
00:00:27,608 --> 00:00:31,578
was wrong with it, and a small
industry of people working around it.

9
00:00:32,262 --> 00:00:35,772
The strange part is that Microsoft
never hid what was happening.

10
00:00:36,642 --> 00:00:39,952
They announced it, in public,
and almost nobody noticed.

11
00:00:40,829 --> 00:00:45,319
This is part four. Part one was
how one browser became a platform.

12
00:00:46,129 --> 00:00:49,379
Part two was what that did to
the people building for it.

13
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:52,579
Part three was why nobody
could switch it off.

14
00:00:53,369 --> 00:00:55,739
This one is the gap underneath all three.

15
00:00:56,469 --> 00:00:58,659
Not what the browser did. Why it stopped.

16
00:01:01,309 --> 00:01:02,509
Start at the top.

17
00:01:03,489 --> 00:01:08,609
In 2001, Internet Explorer 6
arrived with Windows XP, and within

18
00:01:08,689 --> 00:01:11,959
a couple of years it had more
than ninety percent of the market.

19
00:01:12,777 --> 00:01:16,167
Now ask the question that almost
never gets asked about winning.

20
00:01:17,177 --> 00:01:19,967
What do you build next, when
there is nothing left to beat?

21
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:26,037
There was one obvious answer in the
room, and it had been there for years.

22
00:01:26,647 --> 00:01:27,847
The courts.

23
00:01:28,645 --> 00:01:33,395
In May 1998, the American government
and twenty states sued Microsoft.

24
00:01:34,305 --> 00:01:38,655
The heart of the case was this browser,
and how it arrived on your computer.

25
00:01:39,602 --> 00:01:43,592
In 2000, a judge ordered the
company to be broken into two.

26
00:01:44,327 --> 00:01:48,367
And in June 2001, an appeals
court kept the finding that

27
00:01:48,447 --> 00:01:51,857
Microsoft held a monopoly,
and threw out the breakup.

28
00:01:52,701 --> 00:01:55,591
Eight weeks later, Internet
Explorer 6 shipped.

29
00:01:58,491 --> 00:02:02,061
The settlement that followed was
about how Windows had to behave.

30
00:02:02,951 --> 00:02:05,901
Let other people's software
sit in the same places.

31
00:02:06,671 --> 00:02:08,881
Let a computer maker hide the icon.

32
00:02:09,727 --> 00:02:14,437
Nowhere in any of it was there a line that
said: and the browser has to get better.

33
00:02:15,327 --> 00:02:17,347
So the threat of a
breakup ended in the same

34
00:02:17,427 --> 00:02:20,397
season that Internet Explorer 6 arrived.

35
00:02:21,167 --> 00:02:23,637
And then the company said
what it planned to do next.

36
00:02:26,327 --> 00:02:30,187
In an online chat in May
2003, a program manager

37
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:32,677
on the browser team explained the future.

38
00:02:33,484 --> 00:02:37,574
Internet Explorer 6 would be the last
version you could install on its own.

39
00:02:38,324 --> 00:02:41,994
From then on, the browser would
arrive only as part of Windows.

40
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:46,300
Say that slowly, because it
isn't a company admitting defeat.

41
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:50,080
It's a company saying this
is no longer a product.

42
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:53,220
It is a feature of the operating system.

43
00:02:53,991 --> 00:02:56,661
Which is exactly what part
one said had happened.

44
00:02:57,131 --> 00:03:01,421
The difference is that here, they said
it out loud, and put a date on it.

45
00:03:02,111 --> 00:03:04,531
And once the browser is
part of the system, the

46
00:03:04,611 --> 00:03:07,221
browser can only improve
when the system does.

47
00:03:08,151 --> 00:03:10,741
So the next question is
what the system was doing.

48
00:03:13,251 --> 00:03:15,461
The next Windows was going to be enormous.

49
00:03:16,551 --> 00:03:18,641
A new way to draw everything on screen.

50
00:03:19,491 --> 00:03:21,081
A new way to store files.

51
00:03:21,931 --> 00:03:24,301
A new way to write the
applications on top.

52
00:03:25,065 --> 00:03:26,895
It was late. Then it was later.

53
00:03:28,065 --> 00:03:31,265
In 2004 the whole project was
restarted from a different

54
00:03:31,345 --> 00:03:34,475
codebase, and pieces of
it were quietly dropped.

55
00:03:35,296 --> 00:03:38,596
It finally shipped in 2007, more than five

56
00:03:38,676 --> 00:03:41,126
years after the browser
we're talking about.

57
00:03:41,984 --> 00:03:45,694
So the browser was waiting for a
system that was itself being rebuilt.

58
00:03:48,574 --> 00:03:50,144
But where did the people go?

59
00:03:50,834 --> 00:03:54,084
We don't have to guess, because one
of them wrote it down afterwards.

60
00:03:54,987 --> 00:04:00,077
An engineer called Chris Wilson had
worked on Internet Explorer since 1995.

61
00:04:00,527 --> 00:04:04,077
Every major release, all
the way to version 6.

62
00:04:04,855 --> 00:04:08,005
Afterwards he moved onto the
rethink of that Windows platform.

63
00:04:08,755 --> 00:04:12,845
In his words, that is what most
of them moved over to work on.

64
00:04:13,551 --> 00:04:17,421
And he gives a list of reasons,
which is more honest than giving one.

65
00:04:18,169 --> 00:04:21,779
Rich applications inside a
browser hadn't taken off yet.

66
00:04:22,609 --> 00:04:24,259
The dot com bubble had burst.

67
00:04:25,269 --> 00:04:28,499
Security problems were eating
enormous amounts of effort.

68
00:04:29,129 --> 00:04:31,959
They were getting stuck in their
own backwards compatibility.

69
00:04:33,077 --> 00:04:35,337
And they were, in his words, pretty far

70
00:04:35,417 --> 00:04:37,807
ahead of the competition's
released product.

71
00:04:38,655 --> 00:04:41,625
Then he writes the sentence
this whole video is about.

72
00:04:42,485 --> 00:04:47,015
Microsoft does seem to work best with
strong competition motivating it.

73
00:04:47,594 --> 00:04:50,844
That is someone who had led
major parts of Internet Explorer,

74
00:04:51,134 --> 00:04:53,984
saying the browser stopped
because nothing was pushing it.

75
00:04:57,044 --> 00:04:59,904
And here is where the story
gets uncomfortable, because

76
00:04:59,984 --> 00:05:02,994
at that exact moment
Microsoft had another browser.

77
00:05:04,384 --> 00:05:06,294
A different one, with a different engine.

78
00:05:07,126 --> 00:05:11,736
In March 2000, the company released
Internet Explorer 5 for the Macintosh.

79
00:05:12,966 --> 00:05:14,976
It did not run the engine from Windows.

80
00:05:15,926 --> 00:05:19,916
It ran one called Tasman, written
to follow the standards of the day.

81
00:05:20,666 --> 00:05:22,039
Better support for stylesheets.

82
00:05:22,119 --> 00:05:24,696
And no box model bug.

83
00:05:25,479 --> 00:05:27,169
That's the one from part one.

84
00:05:28,039 --> 00:05:30,739
The argument about whether the
width of a box includes its

85
00:05:30,819 --> 00:05:34,389
border, the disagreement that
cost a decade of workarounds.

86
00:05:34,919 --> 00:05:37,289
This browser simply didn't have it.

87
00:05:38,016 --> 00:05:41,666
It also introduced the trick that
modern browsers still use today.

88
00:05:42,596 --> 00:05:45,336
Look at the very first
line of a page, and decide

89
00:05:45,416 --> 00:05:48,246
from it how strictly to
treat everything underneath.

90
00:05:49,186 --> 00:05:52,526
Two modes. One for pages
written to the standards, one

91
00:05:52,606 --> 00:05:55,256
for the pile of pages already out there.

92
00:05:56,186 --> 00:05:59,996
Invented by the team building a
Microsoft browser for Apple computers.

93
00:06:00,889 --> 00:06:03,339
So Microsoft could build
a standards browser.

94
00:06:04,289 --> 00:06:05,759
We know, because they did.

95
00:06:06,577 --> 00:06:10,957
In June 2003 they ended its
development, six weeks after announcing

96
00:06:11,037 --> 00:06:14,807
that Internet Explorer 6 was
the last standalone version.

97
00:06:15,297 --> 00:06:18,187
Two browsers, one company, one summer.

98
00:06:21,117 --> 00:06:23,587
Which changes the story we usually tell.

99
00:06:24,217 --> 00:06:26,307
It isn't Microsoft couldn't fix it.

100
00:06:26,937 --> 00:06:29,447
It's Microsoft no longer had a reason to.

101
00:06:32,557 --> 00:06:34,177
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

102
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:37,107
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

103
00:06:37,997 --> 00:06:39,467
It really helps the channel.

104
00:06:40,249 --> 00:06:42,919
And while the browser stood
still, the web did not.

105
00:06:43,749 --> 00:06:46,159
Here's the part I find genuinely strange.

106
00:06:47,131 --> 00:06:49,311
A lot of what the web became next was

107
00:06:49,391 --> 00:06:51,861
already sitting inside
that frozen browser.

108
00:06:52,795 --> 00:06:54,915
Go back to the late nineties.

109
00:06:55,515 --> 00:06:57,635
A team at Microsoft is
building a version of the

110
00:06:57,715 --> 00:07:01,225
company's email software
that runs inside a browser.

111
00:07:02,216 --> 00:07:05,906
They want a page that can fetch new
mail without loading itself again.

112
00:07:06,696 --> 00:07:08,566
Nothing on the web could do that.

113
00:07:09,216 --> 00:07:11,666
Pages arrived whole, or not at all.

114
00:07:12,343 --> 00:07:14,663
So they shipped a small
component that let a page

115
00:07:14,743 --> 00:07:18,033
make a request on its own
and get the answer back.

116
00:07:18,839 --> 00:07:22,979
It arrived as an ActiveX component,
which tells you everything about where

117
00:07:23,059 --> 00:07:27,709
it came from, and it was named after a
format that was fashionable that year.

118
00:07:28,407 --> 00:07:31,707
That component sat inside
Internet Explorer 5.

119
00:07:31,787 --> 00:07:34,927
Then inside Internet Explorer 6.

120
00:07:35,007 --> 00:07:38,757
Then it sat there for five
years, while nothing happened.

121
00:07:39,536 --> 00:07:41,906
Everyone else eventually built their own.

122
00:07:42,716 --> 00:07:44,916
Other browsers copied the idea, kept the

123
00:07:44,996 --> 00:07:47,826
name, and it is now part
of the standard web.

124
00:07:48,615 --> 00:07:51,465
And then other people picked
it up and built things with it.

125
00:07:52,275 --> 00:07:54,825
A mail service that
never reloaded the page.

126
00:07:55,615 --> 00:07:57,425
A map you could drag with the mouse.

127
00:07:58,273 --> 00:08:01,193
The technique got a name,
and for about three years you

128
00:08:01,273 --> 00:08:05,543
couldn't go near anything about
the web without hearing it. AJAX.

129
00:08:06,691 --> 00:08:11,101
The X in the middle stands for a
format almost nobody ended up using.

130
00:08:11,711 --> 00:08:15,801
The rest of it came out of a component
built for company email in the nineties.

131
00:08:18,450 --> 00:08:21,190
So the browser everybody
was cursing was also

132
00:08:21,270 --> 00:08:24,180
carrying the piece that made
the next web feel alive.

133
00:08:25,031 --> 00:08:26,741
And it wasn't the only piece.

134
00:08:27,709 --> 00:08:30,879
The property that lets a script
throw away a chunk of a page

135
00:08:31,069 --> 00:08:34,839
and put new markup in its place
came from Internet Explorer.

136
00:08:35,729 --> 00:08:38,999
Every framework since is
a descendant of that idea.

137
00:08:39,817 --> 00:08:42,077
The switch that turns part
of a page into something

138
00:08:42,157 --> 00:08:44,847
you can type into came
from Internet Explorer.

139
00:08:45,957 --> 00:08:49,747
Every rich text editor you have
ever typed in sits on top of it.

140
00:08:50,433 --> 00:08:52,763
And the smallest one is my favourite.

141
00:08:53,273 --> 00:08:56,593
When a line of text is too long
for its box, and it ends in three

142
00:08:56,673 --> 00:09:00,243
quiet dots instead of being chopped
off, that came from here too.

143
00:09:02,713 --> 00:09:04,513
Then there is the joke you can check right

144
00:09:04,593 --> 00:09:07,503
now, on the device you
are watching this on.

145
00:09:08,249 --> 00:09:10,779
Open any browser and ask it what it is.

146
00:09:11,409 --> 00:09:13,139
Chrome will tell you it is Mozilla.

147
00:09:14,049 --> 00:09:17,559
Then that it is like Gecko, which
is the engine inside Firefox.

148
00:09:18,529 --> 00:09:19,959
Then it will mention Safari.

149
00:09:21,001 --> 00:09:23,391
Everything except what it actually is.

150
00:09:24,191 --> 00:09:27,911
Every browser spent the nineties
claiming to be the popular one, so that

151
00:09:27,991 --> 00:09:32,021
websites would hand it the good page
instead of the stripped down one.

152
00:09:32,713 --> 00:09:34,963
Internet Explorer did it to Netscape.

153
00:09:35,453 --> 00:09:37,633
Everyone did it back to Internet Explorer.

154
00:09:37,713 --> 00:09:40,903
And nobody has dared to stop since.

155
00:09:41,773 --> 00:09:44,553
Your browser is still lying
about who it is, because

156
00:09:44,633 --> 00:09:47,543
of arguments that were
settled twenty five years ago.

157
00:09:50,263 --> 00:09:52,633
So that is one half of those five years.

158
00:09:53,523 --> 00:09:57,313
The web quietly built itself out
of parts that browser already had.

159
00:09:58,023 --> 00:10:00,633
The other half is what came
in through the same door.

160
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:03,220
Part two ended with a crash.

161
00:10:04,230 --> 00:10:06,430
A broken tag in a web
page could shut a window

162
00:10:06,510 --> 00:10:09,080
that was listing files
on your own computer.

163
00:10:09,981 --> 00:10:14,851
Because the browser was not a program
sitting on the system. It was part of it.

164
00:10:15,481 --> 00:10:17,831
Now run that forwards for five years.

165
00:10:20,221 --> 00:10:22,831
Internet Explorer had a way to extend it.

166
00:10:23,701 --> 00:10:26,141
A small piece of code could
load itself into every

167
00:10:26,221 --> 00:10:29,571
browser window and watch
everything that happened there.

168
00:10:30,239 --> 00:10:31,919
It was built for useful things.

169
00:10:31,999 --> 00:10:33,729
Reading tools.

170
00:10:33,919 --> 00:10:35,589
Download managers.

171
00:10:35,859 --> 00:10:39,409
And it ran with everything you were
allowed to do on your own machine.

172
00:10:40,283 --> 00:10:43,073
So by 2004 this was an industry.

173
00:10:44,027 --> 00:10:46,947
Search toolbars. Shopping toolbars.

174
00:10:47,027 --> 00:10:48,947
Weather toolbars.

175
00:10:49,027 --> 00:10:52,767
Machines with four of them stacked
above the page, eating a third

176
00:10:52,847 --> 00:10:56,337
of the screen, and nobody in the
house remembers installing one.

177
00:10:56,993 --> 00:11:00,283
Some of them were paid for by
whoever wanted your search box.

178
00:11:00,953 --> 00:11:04,223
Some came bundled with something
free you actually wanted.

179
00:11:05,173 --> 00:11:07,403
And some needed no installing at all.

180
00:11:08,079 --> 00:11:11,049
You didn't click anything.
You visited a page.

181
00:11:13,869 --> 00:11:15,879
And there was nowhere to upgrade to.

182
00:11:16,689 --> 00:11:18,949
No newer browser was
coming, because the browser

183
00:11:19,029 --> 00:11:22,199
was part of Windows now,
and Windows was late.

184
00:11:23,055 --> 00:11:24,775
Which is why the fix, when it finally

185
00:11:24,855 --> 00:11:28,105
arrived, came in the
strangest possible shape.

186
00:11:28,847 --> 00:11:30,557
August 2004.

187
00:11:30,707 --> 00:11:32,377
Not a new browser.

188
00:11:33,187 --> 00:11:35,337
A service pack for the operating system.

189
00:11:36,115 --> 00:11:38,285
Inside it, a blocker for pop-up windows.

190
00:11:38,635 --> 00:11:41,545
A manager for all those add-ons.

191
00:11:41,895 --> 00:11:45,585
A bar across the top that told
you when a page wanted something.

192
00:11:46,055 --> 00:11:48,895
And a browser that finally
asked before certain

193
00:11:48,975 --> 00:11:51,265
web controls could reach into the machine.

194
00:11:52,089 --> 00:11:53,699
Sit with that for a second.

195
00:11:54,669 --> 00:11:58,779
To make the browser safer, Microsoft
had to update the operating system.

196
00:11:59,590 --> 00:12:03,100
That is the moment part one stops
being an argument about strategy.

197
00:12:04,070 --> 00:12:06,460
The browser really had
become part of Windows.

198
00:12:07,430 --> 00:12:09,260
The proof is in the shape of the repair.

199
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,610
And it landed exactly the
way part three would predict.

200
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:19,260
Company applications that had
been written for a browser

201
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:22,820
that trusted everything started
asking for permission, and

202
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:25,810
the complaints came in from
the people who had built on it.

203
00:12:26,487 --> 00:12:30,607
Three months later, Firefox reached
version 1, and people who had

204
00:12:30,687 --> 00:12:34,217
never chosen a browser in their
lives started downloading one.

205
00:12:34,925 --> 00:12:39,565
Three months after that, in
February 2005, Bill Gates stood up

206
00:12:39,645 --> 00:12:43,135
at a security conference and
announced a new Internet Explorer.

207
00:12:43,959 --> 00:12:49,159
The reason he gave was security.
Not standards. Not the pixels.

208
00:12:49,239 --> 00:12:51,109
Not ten years of developer complaints.

209
00:12:52,038 --> 00:12:56,578
Internet Explorer 7 arrived
in October 2006, into a web

210
00:12:56,658 --> 00:12:59,728
that had spent five years
learning to work without it.

211
00:13:02,638 --> 00:13:03,928
So why did it stop?

212
00:13:06,128 --> 00:13:08,958
Not because Microsoft couldn't
build a better browser.

213
00:13:09,968 --> 00:13:11,868
There was one, in the same company, in the

214
00:13:11,948 --> 00:13:14,938
same year, with the
famous bug already fixed.

215
00:13:15,869 --> 00:13:17,819
It stopped because it had already won.

216
00:13:18,709 --> 00:13:21,879
The competition that had driven
the first browser war was gone.

217
00:13:22,569 --> 00:13:24,079
The court case was over.

218
00:13:24,729 --> 00:13:26,819
The people had been moved
onto the next thing.

219
00:13:29,219 --> 00:13:33,409
And that is the part worth keeping
from four videos about an old browser.

220
00:13:34,371 --> 00:13:37,141
A product with nothing left
to beat stops changing.

221
00:13:40,331 --> 00:13:43,781
And everything it already has becomes
the ceiling for everyone else.
